items.statement:
For H ∈ {Γ, Γ_par, Γ_Kli(p)}, an H-admissible good polyhedral cone decomposition is an H-admissible rational polyhedral cone decomposition Σ such that:
(1) Σ is projective;
(2) for every cone σ, if W ∈ 𝔆 is minimal with σ ⊂ C(V/W^⊥), and h ∈ H ∩ P_W satisfies hgσ ∩ σ ≠ ∅ for some g ∈ P_{W,h}(A_f^p), then h acts trivially on C(V/W^⊥);
(3) Σ is smooth with respect to X_⋆(T_{W,γ}) if H = Γ, with respect to X_⋆(T_{W,γ,par}) if H = Γ_par, and with respect to X_⋆(T_{W,γ,Kli(p)}) if H = Γ_Kli(p).
Unless explicitly stated otherwise, the cone decompositions used in the paper are good.

Under the hypotheses of §6.3.3, the multiplicative subgroup H = G^m ⊂ G[F] := Ker F over S' extends to a finite flat subgroup scheme H ⊂ G[F] over S. Explicitly:
- the kernel K of V: G[F]^(p) → G[F] (which is also the kernel of F: G^(p)[V] → G^(p^2)[V]) is finite flat of rank p, locally isomorphic to α_p;
- via G/Ker F ≃ G^(p)[V] and the Hodge–Tate sequence, K ≃ Ker(ω_{G^D}[F] →V⋆ ω^(p)_{G^D}[F]), which has rank p by Lemma 6.3.3.1 (ω_{G^D}[F] is a twisted form of α_p^2);
- H := G[F]^(p)/K ↪ G[F], the image of V.
Applied to G^D, the lemma gives a subgroup L ⊂ G^D[F].

(6.3.A) Applying Ext^1_cris(−, O_{S/F_p})_S to G →F G^(p) →V G, with 𝓔 = Ext^1_cris(G, O_{S/F_p})_S, gives a diagram whose columns are the Hodge filtrations 0 → ω → 𝓔 → ω^∨_{(·)^D} → 0 and whose middle row is 𝓔^(p) →F⋆ 𝓔 →V⋆ 𝓔^(p). F⋆ is zero on ω_G^(p), and the map induced by V⋆ on ω^∨_{G^D} → (ω^∨_{G^D})^(p) is zero.
(6.3.B) V⋆: ω_G → ω_G^(p) is compatible with the short exact sequences 0 → ω_{G[F]/H} → ω_G → ω_H → 0 and their Frobenius twists, and it is zero on ω_{G[F]/H}.
Define:
- V⋆_H: ω_H → ω_H^(p), the induced map;
- W: ω^(p)_{G[F]/H} → ω_G^(p)/V⋆(ω_G), inclusion followed by projection;
- Z: ω^∨_{G^D}/F⋆(ω^∨_{G^D})^(p) → ω_G^(p)/V⋆ω_G, induced by V⋆ of (6.3.A).

Let G be the semi-abelian scheme over X and G' the one over X_par. For n ≥ 1, X_n → Spec Z/p^n is the reduction of X mod p^n, and X_{par,n} that of X_par. For r ∈ {0,1,2}:
- X_n^{=r} is the locally closed subset of X_n where the multiplicative rank of G is exactly r;
- X_n^{≤r} is the closed subset where it is at most r (printed 'less than r');
- X_n^{≥r} is the open subscheme where it is at least r (printed 'greater than r').
X^{=r}_{par,n}, X^{≤r}_{par,n} and X^{≥r}_{par,n} are defined in the same way using G'.

items.note:
Lemma 9.1.1.1 is about the missing deep Klingen tower. Its proof also uses:
- the finite flat replacement G̃[p^m];
- the unique lifting into étale groups (missing, R07.1 route);
- the Grassmannian of subgroups of the Frobenius kernel G[F^m], with the Verschiebung section.
Frobenius and Verschiebung are planned only for elliptic curves (Tau Ceti ModularCurves 7E, PD-3) and, over perfect fields, in FiniteFlatGroupsAndIntegralPadicHodgeTheory:R07.2. The lemma is used right away. §9.1.2 (p. 55) restates it for the square comparing the reductions mod p^{n−1} and mod p^n, whose vertical maps are étale and affine. In §9.2 (p. 55) the affineness of the transition maps is what makes the inverse limit 𝔛^{≥1}_Kli(p^∞) exist.

𝔉^κ = (π_*O_{𝔍𝔊(p^∞)} ⊗̂ Λ)^{Z_p^×} lives on the missing Igusa tower. The nearest planned constructions are in OverconvergentAutomorphicForms:
- O1: equivariant sheaves from cocycles;
- O7: Hilbert Igusa forms;
- O8: Siegel coefficient sheaves 'on the proven anticanonical domains'.
None builds this Λ-adic sheaf on the Siegel p-rank ≥ 1 Klingen locus, or its interpolation map (missing). The introduction (§1, p. 3) states the interpolation property: 𝔉^κ(−D) interpolates the sheaves {Ω^{(k,0)}(−D)}_{k∈Z≥0}, in that for every k ∈ Z_{≥0} there is a canonical map Ω^{(k,0)}(−D) → 𝔉^κ(−D) ⊗_{Λ,k} Z_p. On p. 4 it describes 𝔉^κ as obtained by p-adically interpolating the powers of ω_{H_∞}, which is possible because the Hodge–Tate map gives a Z_p^×-reduction of the G_m-torsor ω_{H_∞}. In the body the interpolation map appears at finite level as the §9.4 surjection Ω^{(k,0)} → (ω_{H_n})^{⊗k} ≅ F^k_{n,n} (see highest-weight-projection-and-kernel-K-Omega). The introduction (p. 3) also describes the complex M of Theorem 1.1 as the U-ordinary part of the cohomology of 𝔉^κ ⊗ Ω^{(0,2)}(−D) on 𝔛^{≥1}_Kli(p)_K. So §1, pp. 3–4 is a second locator.

Several inputs are planned:
- generic-fibre Siegel moduli at arbitrary level (PELModuli:M1 'Over characteristic zero define the all-primes adelic variant'; M5 'Construct Siegel moduli for every dimension');
- characteristic-0 toroidal compactifications and semi-abelian degenerations (ShimuraCompactifications:C2–C4);
- analytification (AdicSpacesPartII:R1);
- Weil pairings (AbelianSchemesAndArithmeticModuli:A3);
- integral differentials of p-divisible groups (HodgeTateAndCanonicalSubgroups:T0).
The following are not planned: the moduli description by the chain G → G′ → G with H = Ker(G → G′)^⊥ and G″ = (G′)^t on the analytic toroidal compactification; the O^+-lattices ω^+_G, ω^+_{G″}; δ_H; and the identity v_x(δ_H) = deg H_x, which needs Fargues' degree (missing).

items.statement:
- Let 𝒴_Kli(p) ⊂ 𝒳_Kli(p) be the open locus where G is an abelian scheme.
- For n ∈ N, C_n|_{𝒴_Kli(p)} is the moduli space of triples (G, H, L_n), where:
  - (G, H) ∈ 𝒴_Kli(p);
  - L_n ⊂ G[p^{2n}] is totally isotropic (printed: G[p^n]; sourceIssues E124);
  - L_n is étale-locally isomorphic to (Z/p^n Z)^2 ⊕ Z/p^{2n} Z;
  - L_n ∩ H = {0}.
- t_{n,1}(G, H, L_n) = (G, H) and t_{n,2}(G, H, L_n) = (G/L_n, (H + L_n)/L_n).
- C_n is the n-fold iterate of C_1, which is the correspondence C of §13.2.1.
- There are smooth polyhedral cone decompositions Σ, Σ′, toroidal compactifications C_n = C_{n,Σ′} and 𝒳_Kli(p) = 𝒳_Kli(p)_Σ, and maps t_{n,1}, t_{n,2}: C_{n,Σ′} → 𝒳_Kli(p)_Σ extending the maps above.

Let ε < 1 and f ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F) with Uf = af for some a ≠ 0. Then for every ε′ with ε > ε′ > 0 there is a unique g ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_{ε′}, F) such that:
- Ug = ag;
- res_{ε′,ε} g = f.

Let ε < 1 and f ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F). Let P = X^m + a_{m−1} X^{m−1} + … + a_0 ∈ O[X] have degree m and a_0 ≠ 0, and assume P(U) f = 0. Then for every ε′ with ε > ε′ > 0 there is a unique g ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_{ε′}, F) such that:
- P(U) g = 0;
- res_{ε′,ε} g = f.

Let x: Spa(K, O_K) → C_n be a point with |δ_{L_n}|_x ≤ |p^{3n−α}|_x (α ∈ R) and let k, r ≥ 0. Then:
- the map Ω^+_{G/L_n}|_x → Ω^+_G|_x factors through p^{n−α} Ω^+_G|_x;
- the map Sym^k Ω^+_{G/L_n} ⊗ det^r Ω^+_{G/L_n}|_x → Sym^k Ω^+_G ⊗ det^r Ω^+_G|_x factors through p^{k(n−α) + r(3n−α)} Sym^k Ω^+_G ⊗ det^r Ω^+_G|_x.

Let 0 < ε < ε′, n ≥ 1, F ∈ {Ω^{(k,r)}, Ω^{(k,r)}(−D)} with k, r ≥ 0, and N = n(2r + k − 3 − 2ε′(r + k)).

There is a morphism of sheaves on 𝒳_Kli(p)
  U^n_{ε,0}: (t_{n,1})_* (t_{n,2})^* (F^{++}|_{𝒳_Kli(p)_ε}) → F/p^N F^{++}
such that:
- (i) composed with F/p^N F^{++} → (F/p^N F^{++})|_{𝒳_Kli(p)_ε}, it is U^n_{ε,ε} reduced mod p^N F^{++};
- (ii) precomposed with (t_{n,1})_*(t_{n,2})^* F^{++} → (t_{n,1})_*(t_{n,2})^*(F^{++}|_{𝒳_Kli(p)_ε}), it is the normalized U^n: (t_{n,1})_*(t_{n,2})^* F^{++} → p^{−3n} F^{++} ⊂ F followed by F → F/p^N F^{++}.

Let (k, r) ∈ Z_{≥0} × Z_{≥0} (r ≥ 0 is the standing hypothesis of §13.3 and is not restated in §14; sourceIssues E128), 0 < ε < 1 (printed only 'ε > 0'; sourceIssues E132), F ∈ {Ω^{(k,r)}, Ω^{(k,r)}(−D)}, i ≥ 0. Let f ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F) with Uf = af, where v(a) < 2r + k − 3. Then there is a projective system (f_n) ∈ lim_n H^i(𝒳_Kli(p), F/p^n F^{++}) such that:
- U(f_n) = a(f_n);
- res_{0,ε}(f_n) is the image of f in lim_n H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F/p^n F^{++}).

Let 0 < ε < 1 (ε is unquantified in print), F ∈ {Ω^{(k,r)}, Ω^{(k,r)}(−D)} and f ∈ H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F). Let P = X^m + a_{m−1} X^{m−1} + … + a_0 ∈ O[X] have degree m, with P(U) f = 0 and v(a) < 2r + k − 3 for every root a ∈ C of P (so a_0 ≠ 0). Then there is (f_n) ∈ lim_n H^i(𝒳_Kli(p), F/p^n F^{++}) such that:
- P(U)(f_n) = 0;
- res_{0,ε}(f_n) is the image of f in lim_n H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, F/p^n F^{++}).

Let (k, r) ∈ Z_{≥0} × Z_{≥0} (the paper states r ≥ 0 only at the start of §13.3, p. 88; sourceIssues E128), i ≥ 0 and ε ∈ [0, 1[ ∩ Q. The restriction map
  H^i(𝒳_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,r)})^{<k+2r−3} → H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, Ω^{(k,r)})^{<k+2r−3}
is bijective. Here ^{<k+2r−3} is the part of slope < k + 2r − 3 for the normalized operator U of §14.4.2. The same holds with Ω^{(k,r)}(−D) (cuspidal cohomology).

items.note:
The classicality theorems the atlas plans are for other settings:
- PadicFamilies:L2, 'Prove the classicality/control theorem in the precise small-slope range', for modular symbols;
- ModularSymbolsPadicLFunctions:L2, with h < w−1;
- AutomorphicPadicLFunctions:L2, BSW Theorem 8.7;
- AutomorphicGaloisRepresentationsPartII:AG2.3, definite unitary groups.
None treats higher coherent cohomology of the Siegel threefold. The OverconvergentAutomorphicForms document excludes it: 'Eigenvarieties, general finite-slope classicality, and higher Coleman cohomology are not endpoints of this roadmap.' The introduction states the result without quantifiers (§1, pp. 5–6): small slope cohomology classes are classical. It is proved by adapting the analytic continuation and gluing argument of Kassaei [38], which has to be rephrased at the sheaf level because higher cohomology classes cannot be glued. The overconvergent form is Cor. 14.7.1 (p. 100), with slope bounds min{k+2r−3, k−2} (isomorphism in all degrees) and min{k+2r−3, k+1} (isomorphism on H^0, injection on H^1).

items.statement:
The natural map
  H^0(𝒳_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,r)})^{<min{k+2r−3, k+1}} → H^0(†, k, r)^{<min{k+2r−3, k+1}}
is an isomorphism; likewise for cuspidal cohomology.

The natural map
  H^1(𝒳_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,r)})^{<min{k+2r−3, k+1}} → H^1(†, k, r)^{<min{k+2r−3, k+1}}
is injective; likewise for cuspidal cohomology.

The C-vector space H^i(𝒳^{≥1}_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,2)}(−D)) has a 0-slope decomposition in the sense of §13.1.1:
- f H^i(…) = H^i(…)^{=0}, the slope-0 part;
- (1 − f) H^i(…) = H^i(…)^{>0}, the part of slope > 0.

items.note:
The good-level integral minimal compactification X^⋆ is planned (ShimuraCompactifications:C5: 'the Hodge-line positivity and graded-section finite-generation statements that yield the integral minimal compactification'). Not planned:
- the first and second Hasse invariants Ha, Ha′ of the Siegel threefold (PAPER-CALEGARI-GERAGHTY-20 routes h and h̃ to the proposed SiegelModularFormsModPn);
- X^⋆_Kli(p), and the descent of det ω_G → det ω_{G′} to it;
- Lan's vanishing (missing);
- the Čech complex and its overconvergent subcomplex, and the topology in footnote 25. The introduction (§1, p. 3) also records the covering: the image of 𝔛^{≥1}_Kli(p)_K in the minimal compactification is covered by two affines, 'and this explains why the complex M is supported in two degrees'. External input: Uses [50] K.-W. Lan, Integral models of toroidal compactifications with projective cone decompositions, IMRN 2017, Thm. 8.9.

items.statement:
For every k ≥ 0 and ε ∈ ]0, 1[ ∩ Q, the restriction map f H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, Ω^{(k,2)}(−D)) → f H^i(𝒳^{≥1}_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,2)}(−D)) is:
- injective for i = 0;
- surjective for i = 1.

For k ≥ 0 and i ∈ {0, 1}:
- d_i(k) = dim_C f H^i(𝒳^{≥1}_Kli(p), Ω^{(k,2)}(−D));
- d_i^†(k) = dim_C f H^i(𝒳_Kli(p)_ε, Ω^{(k,2)}(−D)) for any ε ∈ [0, 1[ ∩ Q (independent of ε by Thm. 14.7.1 and Prop. 14.5.2);
- d_i^{†,′}(k) = dim_C H^i_cusp(†, k, 2)^{=0} (printed without '=0'; sourceIssues E140).

